  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot with dry weather today. Humidity briefly \ntrends down tonight before returning to higher levels Mon-Wed. \nRelief from the heat comes late week.\n\nExpecting today to remain generally dry as overall flow returns\nto a zonal, westerly pattern. Temperatures remain in the upper \n80s, near 90s, today as 925mb temps continue to hover around \n+22C. Expecting a weak cold front to push through later this \nevening, bringing a slightly drier airmass overnight. \nUnfortunately, dewpoints will likely remain elevated throughout \nthe daytime hours, prolonging humid conditions, before trending \ninto the low 60s tonight. The overall pattern is expected to \nlinger through mid-week, leading daily high temps to be in the \nupper 80s and dewpoints to tick back up into the upper 60s, \nperiodically near 70, through Wednesday before relief finally \nmoves into southern New England... high temps by Friday near 80 \nwith dewpoints dropping into the 50s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Overall unsettled weather continues through \nmuch of the week with daily shower and thunderstorm chances.\n\nA low pressure system sitting over the Hudson Bay will slowly \nmove south through the James Bay this week, pushing several \nupper-level shortwaves into the Eastern Townships and New \nEngland. These will likely be the catalyst for several days of \ndiurnal shower and thunderstorm chances. Similarly to what we've\nseen over the last week, the models are struggling to resolve \nconvection, leading to greater uncertainty in exactly where and \nwhen storms could fire. Southern New England will see several \nstretches of dry weather between these shortwaves, so not \nexpecting rain and thunder all the time, but there will be \nstorms to dodge here-and-there.",
